PART 2 
Algorithm 2: Task Text Parser

The algorithm converts free-form text entered by a user into a structured Task object. It looks for special symbols to identify task information:

! → priority
@ → tag
# → due date
The algorithm first sets default values, extracts the priority, tags and date information, removes those markers from the title, converts the information into the correct format, and finally creates and returns a Task object.
The getNextWeekday() helper is responsible for calculating the next occurrence of a weekday such as Monday or Friday.

The main insight I gained is that this is a rule-based text parsing algorithm. It does not simply store the text entered by the user; it interprets specific patterns and converts them into structured data.

I learned that:

Regular expressions are used to find priority, tag and date markers.
Default values are used when the user does not provide certain information.
The algorithm separates extracting information from creating the task.
The @, !, and # symbols act like a small command language for creating tasks.
getNextWeekday() separates date calculation from the main parsing logic.
The original text is retained while the title variable is cleaned.
If multiple date markers are provided, the first recognized date is used.
A weekday such as #friday refers to the next Friday, even if today is already Friday.

I would explain it like this:

This algorithm allows a user to create a task using a simple sentence. It scans the sentence for special markers that represent task information. ! represents priority, @ represents tags, and # represents a due date. It extracts those values, removes the markers from the task title, converts the values into the appropriate formats, and then creates a Task object. A separate helper function calculates the next occurrence of a requested weekday.

For example:

"Buy milk @shopping !2 #tomorrow"

        ↓

Title     = "Buy milk"
Priority  = MEDIUM
Tags      = ["shopping"]
Due Date  = Tomorrow

        ↓

      Task object

